window.docContents[215].resolve({"/Cliques/#section-triangle-free-mycielskians":{"contents":"We start with the promised proof that the Mycielskian of a triangle-free graph is\ntriangle-free, before moving on to the more general result that the Mycielskian\npreserves the clique number (of any finite graph with at least one edge).\n\nsection mycielskian_cliquenum\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\ntheorem CliqueFree.mycielskian (h : G.CliqueFree 3) : mμ[G].CliqueFree 3 := by\n  intro t ht\n  -- classical provides the DecidableEq V instance required in the proof\n  classical\n  rw [is3Clique_iff] at ht\n  obtain ⟨a, b, c, hab, hac, hbc, _⟩ := ht\n  rcases a with _ | (a₁ | a₂) <;>\n  rcases b with _ | (b₁ | b₂) <;>\n  rcases c with _ | (c₁ | c₂) <;>\n  -- 27 goals, but 23 are easily dispatched by simp only since they either assert\n  -- the existence of loops; edges inside the \"right\" copy of V; or edges from the\n  -- \"left\" copy of V to none, all of which do not exist in mμ[G].\n  simp only [mycielskian_adj] at hab hac hbc <;>\n    -- only 4 goals remain!\n    -- In each goal the local context contains three adjacency hypotheses\n    -- that assert the existence of a triangle in G, contradicting our\n    -- assumption that G is triangle-free.\n    exact h _ <| is3Clique_triple_iff.mpr ⟨hab, hac, hbc⟩\n\n\nHence the iterated Mycielskian of any triangle-free graph is also triangle-free.\n\ntheorem CliqueFree.iterMycielskian (n : ℕ) (h : G.CliqueFree 3) :\n    (mμ^n G).CliqueFree 3 := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => exact h\n  | succ _ ih => exact ih.mycielskian\n\n\nIn particular every iterated Mycielskian of the single edge K[2] is triangle-free.\nUp to isomorphism this sequence begins K[2], C[5], grotzschGraph: we have\nmμ[K[2]] ≃g C[5] (mycielskianCompleteTwoIsoCycleFive), and applying\nIso.mapMycielskian to this gives mμ^2 K[2] ≃g grotzschGraph.\n\ntheorem cliqueFree_three_iterMycielskian_completeGraph_two (n : ℕ) :\n    (mμ^n K[2]).CliqueFree 3 :=\n  CliqueFree.iterMycielskian n (by decide)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques\u0009Clique and Independence Number of the Mycielskian","header":"6.7.1. Triangle-Free Mycielskians","id":"/Cliques/#section-triangle-free-mycielskians"},"/Maps/#chapter-maps":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nMaps, in particular graph homomorphisms, are a fundamental concept in graph theory\nbut they are often glossed over in informal treatments. In Lean they play a much more\ncentral role. For example, to say that a graph contains an induced 5-cycle we need\nto talk about embeddings of C[5] into the graph. Underlying this is the fact that\nany SimpleGraph V has a fixed vertex type V, so to even express the idea that a\ngraph G contains a copy of H, when G and H have different vertex types,\nrequires the use of a map.\n\nIn this chapter we will study mappings between graphs. We first review functions,\nembeddings, and equivalences of types, and then the construction SimpleGraph.comap,\nwhich pulls a graph back along a function (it is used to define induced subgraphs).\nNext we introduce graph homomorphisms (→g), graph embeddings (↪g), and graph\nisomorphisms (≃g), together with SimpleGraph.Copy, the type of injective graph\nhomomorphisms. We then look at SimpleGraph.map, which pushes a graph forward along\na function, and at the natural maps associated with our running examples, the\nhypercube and the Mycielskian. Next we use graph automorphisms to define\nvertex-transitivity, a notion that recurs throughout the guide.\n\nThe chapter ends with two more advanced sections, on the tensor product of graphs\nand on claw-free graphs, where we characterize when the line graph of a graph\ncontains a triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"5. Maps","id":"/Maps/#chapter-maps"}});